window.CMA_QS=[{"id":"v2-su17-q932","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) A major impact of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act of 1977 is\nthat registrants subject to the Securities Exchange Act of 1934\nare now required to","options":["Keep records that reflect the transactions and dispositions of\nassets and to maintain a system of internal accounting\ncontrols.","Provide access to records by authorized agencies of the\nfederal government.","Prepare financial statements in accord with international\naccounting standards.","Produce full, fair, and accurate periodic reports on foreign\ncommerce and/or foreign political party affiliations."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The main purpose of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act of\n1977 is to prevent bribery by firms that do business in foreign\ncountries. A major ramification is that it requires all companies\n\nMeasures | Subunit 5: Transfer Pricing\nthat must register with the SEC under the Securities Exchange\nAct of 1934 to maintain adequate accounting records and a\nsystem of internal accounting control.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q960","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) Risk assessment is a process","options":["Designed to identify potential events that may affect the entity.","That establishes policies and procedures to accomplish\ninternal control objectives.","Of identifying and capturing information in a timely fashion.","That assesses the quality of internal control throughout the\nyear."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Every organization faces risks, that is, unforeseen obstacles to\nthe pursuit of its objectives. Risks take many forms and can\noriginate from within or from outside the organization. Risk\nassessment is the process whereby management identifies\nthe organization’s vulnerabilities.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q961","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) There are three components of audit risk: inherent risk, control\nrisk, and detection risk. Inherent risk is\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","options":["The susceptibility of an assertion to a material misstatement,\nassuming that there are no related internal control structure\npolicies or procedures.","The risk that the auditor may unknowingly fail to appropriately\nmodify his or her opinion on financial statements that are\nmaterially misstated.","The risk that a material misstatement that could occur in an\nassertion will not be prevented or detected on a timely basis by\nthe entity’s internal control structure policies or procedures.","The risk that the auditor will not detect a material misstatement\nthat exists in an assertion.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"A","explanation":"According to AU 312, “Inherent risk is the susceptibility of an\nassertion to a material misstatement, assuming that there are\nno related internal control structure policies or procedures.\nThe risk of such misstatement is greater for some assertions\nand related balances or classes than for others.” Unlike\ndetection risk, inherent risk and control risk “are independent\nof the audit.” Furthermore, inherent risk and control risk are\ninversely related to detection risk. Thus, the lower the inherent\nrisk, the higher the acceptable detection risk.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q962","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) Which of the following is not a component of internal control?","options":["Control risk.","Monitoring.","Information and communication.","The control environment."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The five components of internal control described in\nCOSO’s Internal Control -- Integrated Framework are control\nenvironment, risk assessment, control activities, information\nand communication, and monitoring.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q963","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) The primary responsibility for establishing and maintaining\ninternal control rests with","options":["The external auditor.","Management.","The controller.","The treasurer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Establishing and maintaining internal control is the\nresponsibility of management. Internal control is intended to\nprovide reasonable assurance that the entity’s objectives are\nachieved. Achievement of these objectives is the basic\nfunction of management.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q964","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) Some account balances, such as those for pensions or leases,\nare the results of complex calculations. The susceptibility to\nmaterial misstatements in these types of accounts is defined\nas","options":["Audit risk.","Detection risk.","Sampling risk.","Inherent risk."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Inherent risk is the susceptibility of an assertion to a material\nmisstatement in the absence of related controls. This risk is\ngreater for some assertions and related balances or classes\nthan others. For example, complex calculations are more likely\nto be misstated than simple ones, and cash is more likely to\nbe stolen than an inventory of coal. Inherent risk exists\nindependently of the audit.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q965","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) Basic to a proper control environment are the quality and\nintegrity of personnel who must perform the prescribed\nprocedures. Which is not a factor in providing for competent\npersonnel?","options":["Segregation of duties.","Hiring practices.","Training programs.","Performance evaluations."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Human resource policies and practices are a factor in the\ncontrol environment component of internal control. They affect\nthe entity’s ability to employ sufficient competent personnel to\naccomplish its objectives. Policies and practices include those\nfor hiring, orientation, training, evaluating, promoting,\ncompensating, and remedial actions. Although control\nactivities based on the segregation of duties are important to\ninternal control, they do not in themselves promote employee\ncompetence.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q966","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) When management of the sales department has the\nopportunity to override the system of internal controls of the\naccounting department, a weakness exists in\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","options":["Risk management.","Information and communication.","Monitoring.","The control environment."],"answer":"D","explanation":"An organization’s control environment encompasses the\nattitudes and actions of the board of directors and upper\nmanagement regarding the significance of control, i.e., the\n“tone at the top.” One of the components of the control\nenvironment is the assignment of authority and responsibility.\nFor example, management defines key areas of authority and\nresponsibility by placing the information technology, financial\naccounting, and treasury functions under separate officers.\nWhen the management of one department can override the\ninternal controls of another, authority and responsibility have\nnot been properly assigned.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q967","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) One of the financial statement auditor’s major concerns is to\nascertain whether internal control is designed to provide\nreasonable assurance that","options":["Profit margins are maximized, and operational efficiency is\noptimized.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","The chief accounting officer reviews all accounting\ntransactions.","Corporate morale problems are addressed immediately and\neffectively.","Financial reporting is reliable."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Internal control is designed to provide reasonable assurance\nof the achievement of objectives in the categories of (1)\nreliability of financial reporting, (2) effectiveness and efficiency\nof operations, and (3) compliance with laws and regulations.\nControls relevant to a financial statement audit ordinarily\npertain to the objective of preparing external financial\nstatements that are fairly presented in conformity with GAAP\nor another comprehensive basis of accounting.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q968","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) Directors, management, external auditors, and internal\nauditors all play important roles in creating proper control\nprocesses. Senior management is primarily responsible for","options":["Overseeing the establishment, administration, and assessment\nof control processes.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Reviewing the reliability and integrity of financial and\noperational information.","Ensuring that external and internal auditors oversee the\nadministration of the system of risk management and control\nprocesses.","Implementing and monitoring controls designed by the board\nof directors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Senior management’s role is to oversee the establishment,\nadministration, and assessment of the system of risk\nmanagement and control processes. Among the\nresponsibilities of the organization’s line managers is the\nassessment of the control processes in their respective areas.\nInternal auditors provide varying degrees of assurance about\nthe effectiveness of the risk management and control\nprocesses in select activities and functions of the organization.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q969","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) The graphic portrayal of the flow of data and the information\nprocessing of a system, including computer hardware,\nis best displayed in a","options":["Data-flow diagram.","System flowchart.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Gantt chart.","Program flowchart."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A system flowchart is a graphic analysis of a data processing\napplication, usually prepared by a systems analyst. It is\ngeneral and stresses flows of data, not computer program\nlogic. A program flowchart is a graphic representation of the\ndetailed steps and logic of an individual computer program.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q970","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) A director of a corporation is best characterized as a(n)","options":["Agent.","Trustee.","Fiduciary.","Principal."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Officers and employees as well as directors are fiduciaries\nwith regard to the corporation. They owe a duty of loyalty,\ngood faith, and fair dealing when transacting business with or\non behalf of the company. This duty requires full disclosure of\nany personal interest in transactions with the corporation,\navoidance of conflicts of interest and the making of secret\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nprofits, and placing the corporate interest ahead of personal\ngain.","sectionId":"17.1"},{"id":"v2-su17-q971","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) Internal control is a process designed to provide reasonable\nassurance regarding the achievement of objectives related to","options":["Reporting.","Operations.","Compliance.","All of the answers are correct."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The COSO model for internal control establishes control\nobjectives for operations, reporting, and compliance.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q972","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) The top-down approach to the audit of internal control over\nfinancial reporting can best be described as beginning","options":["At the financial statement level, focusing on entity-level\ncontrols, and working down to significant accounts and\ndisclosures and their relevant assertions.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","With interviewing top management and observing the actions\nof top management with respect to the entity’s control\nenvironment.","With considerations of the controls over assets and their\nrelated transactions, progressing to controls over liabilities and\nto controls over equities.","By identifying significant accounts and disclosures for each\nassertion that has a reasonable possibility of containing a\nmaterial misstatement."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The top-down approach to the audit of internal controls over\nfinancial reporting is best described as beginning at the\nfinancial statement level, focusing on entity-level controls (the\n“top”), and working down to significant accounts and\ndisclosures and their relevant assertions (the “bottom”). The\ntop-down approach is also known as the risk-based approach.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q973","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) The external auditor has been examining the operation for\nefficiency and control. The auditor has asked the accounting\nmanager that oversees the payment process to provide\ndocumentation of the process to see how the payments move\nthrough the process. Which of the following statements\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nrepresents the best documentation that the accounting\nmanager could provide?","options":["A matrix analysis because it allows the auditor to see the\noperation and identify missing steps.","A flowchart because it allows the auditor to see the operation\nand identify missing steps.","A matrix analysis because it shows risks and controls to\nensure that all risks are covered.","A flowchart because it shows risks and controls to ensure that\nall risks are covered."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A flowchart can represent a process using symbols. It is a\nuseful tool for auditors who want to gain an understanding of\ninternal control. The auditors can become familiar with\npayments processing by following the steps in a flowchart,\nwhich enables them to identify missing steps.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q974","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) When documenting the understanding of a client’s internal\ncontrol, the independent auditor sometimes uses a systems\nflowchart, which can best be described as a","options":["Pictorial presentation of the flow of instructions in a client’s\ninternal computer system.","Diagram that clearly indicates an organization’s internal\nreporting structure.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Graphic illustration of the flow of operations that is used to\nreplace the auditor’s internal control questionnaire.","Symbolic representation of a system or series of sequential\nprocesses."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A systems flowchart is a symbolic representation of the flow of\ndocuments and procedures through a series of steps in the\naccounting process of the client’s organization.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q975","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) An advantage of using systems flowcharts to document\ninformation about internal control instead of using internal\ncontrol questionnaires is that systems flowcharts","options":["Identify internal control weaknesses more prominently.","Provide a visual depiction of clients’ activities.","Indicate whether control procedures are operating effectively.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Reduce the need to observe clients’ employees performing\nroutine tasks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Systems flowcharts provide a visual representation of a series\nof sequential processes, that is, of a flow of documents, data,\nand operations. In many instances a flowchart is preferable to\na questionnaire because a picture is usually more easily\ncomprehended.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q976","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) Internal controls are designed to provide reasonable\nassurance that","options":["Material errors or fraud will be prevented or detected and\ncorrected within a timely period by employees in the course of\nperforming their assigned duties.","Management’s plans have not been circumvented by worker\ncollusion.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","The internal auditing department’s guidance and oversight of\nmanagement’s performance is accomplished economically and\nefficiently.","Management’s planning, organizing, and directing processes\nare properly evaluated."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Reasonable assurance is provided when cost-effective actions\nare taken to restrict deviations to a tolerable level. This\nimplies, for example, that material errors and improper or\nillegal acts will be prevented or detected and corrected within\na timely period by employees in the normal course of\nperforming their assigned duties. The cost-benefit relationship\nis considered by management during the design of systems.\nThe potential loss associated with any exposure or risk is\nweighed against the cost to control it.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q977","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) An auditor reviews and adapts a systems flowchart to\nunderstand the flow of information in the processing of cash\nreceipts. The flowchart","options":["Depicts specific control procedures used, such as edit tests\nand batch control reconciliations.","Is a good guide to potential segregation of duties.","Is generally kept up to date for systems changes.","Depicts only computer processing, not manual processing.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Systems flowcharts are overall graphic analyses of the flow of\ndata and the processing steps in an information system.\nAccordingly, they can be used to represent segregation of\nduties and the transfer of data between different segments in\nthe organization.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q978","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) Which of the following are considered control environment\nfactors?\nPersonnel Policies\nDetection Risk and Practices","options":["Yes Yes","Yes No","No Yes","No No\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Human resource policies and practices are part of the control\nenvironment. They relate to hiring, orientation, training,\nevaluating, counseling, promoting, compensating, and\nremedial actions. The control environment is the component\nthat sets the tone of an organization, influencing the control\nconsciousness of its people. It is the foundation for the other\ncomponents. Detection risk is not part of the control\nenvironment. It is the risk that the audit procedures will fail to\ndetect material misstatements. Thus, detection risk is a\nfunction of the effectiveness of the procedures used by the\nauditor.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q979","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) Corporate directors, management, external auditors, and\ninternal auditors all play important roles in creating a proper\ncontrol environment. Top management is primarily responsible\nfor","options":["Establishing a proper environment and specifying an overall\ninternal control structure.","Reviewing the reliability and integrity of financial information\nand the means used to collect and report such information.","Ensuring that external and internal auditors adequately monitor\nthe control environment.","Implementing and monitoring controls designed by the board\nof directors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"According to SIAS 1, “Management plans, organizes, and\ndirects in such a fashion as to provide reasonable assurance\nthat established goals and objectives will be achieved.” Also,\n“Management establishes and maintains an environment that\nfosters control.”","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q980","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) Certain management characteristics may heighten the\nauditor’s concern about the risk of material misstatements. The\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\ncharacteristic that is least likely to cause concern is that\nmanagement","options":["Operating and financing decisions are made by numerous\nindividuals.","Commits to unduly aggressive forecasts.","Has an excessive interest in increasing the entity’s stock price\nthrough use of unduly aggressive accounting practices.","Is interested in inappropriate methods of minimizing earnings\nfor tax purposes."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The auditor would be concerned if the decision process were\ndominated by one individual or a small group. In that case,\ncompensating controls, e.g., effective oversight by the audit\ncommittee, reduce risk.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q981","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) A restaurant food chain has over 680 restaurants. All food\norders for each restaurant are required to be input into an\nelectronic device which records all food orders by food servers\nand transmits the order to the kitchen for preparation. All food\nservers are responsible for collecting cash for all their orders\nand must turn in cash at the end of their shift equal to the sales\nvalue of food ordered for their I.D. number. The manager then\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nreconciles the cash received for the day with the computerized\nrecord of food orders generated. All differences are\ninvestigated immediately by the restaurant. Corporate\nheadquarters has established monitoring controls to determine\nwhen an individual restaurant might not be recording all its\nrevenue and transmitting the applicable cash to the corporate\nheadquarters. Which one of the following would be\nthe best example of a monitoring control?","options":["The restaurant manager reconciles the cash received with the\nfood orders recorded on the computer.","All food orders must be entered on the computer, and\nsegregation of duties is maintained between the food servers\nand the cooks.","Management prepares a detailed analysis of gross margin per\nstore and investigates any store that shows a significantly\nlower gross margin.","Cash is transmitted to corporate headquarters on a daily basis."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Monitoring is a process that assesses the quality of internal\ncontrol over time. It involves assessment by appropriate\npersonnel of the design and operation of controls and the\ntaking of corrective action. Monitoring can be done through\nongoing activities or separate evaluations. Ongoing monitoring\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nprocedures are built into the normal recurring activities of an\nentity and include regular management and supervisory\nactivities. Thus, analysis of gross margin data and\ninvestigation of significant deviations is a monitoring process.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q982","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) Audit risk consists of inherent risk, control risk, and detection\nrisk. Which of the following statements is true?","options":["Cash is more susceptible to theft than an inventory of coal\nbecause it has a greater inherent risk.","The risk that material misstatement will not be prevented or\ndetected on a timely basis by internal control can be reduced\nto zero by effective controls.","Detection risk is a function of the efficiency of an auditing\nprocedure.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","The existing levels of inherent risk, control risk, and detection\nrisk can be changed at the discretion of the auditor."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inherent risk is the susceptibility of an assertion to material\nmisstatement in the absence of related controls. Some\nassertions and related balances or classes of transactions\nhave greater inherent risk. Thus, cash has a greater inherent\nrisk than less liquid assets.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q983","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) The basic concepts implicit in internal accounting controls\ninclude the following:\n The cost of the system should not exceed benefits\nexpected to be attained.\n The overall impact of the control procedure should not\nhinder operating efficiency.\nWhich one of the following internal accounting controls\nrecognizes these two factors?","options":["Limitations.","Management responsibility.","Methods of data processing.","Reasonable assurance."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The benefits of internal controls must always exceed the costs\nof implementing them. A system of absolute assurance could\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nonly be implemented at prohibitively great expense.\nReasonable assurance is a cost beneficial solution.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q984","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) The COSO model component of Risk Assessment focuses on","options":["Identifying the risks in all areas and establishing the\nvulnerability of the organization.","The attitude of management at all levels toward operations\nand the concept of risk.","The controls such as approvals and documentation that\naddresses an organization’s risk.","The timely communication of information relative to identified\nrisks to management."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Risk Assessment in the COSO model is the identification and\nanalysis of relevant risks to achievement of the objectives. It\nforms a basis for determining how the risks should be\nmanaged.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q985","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) The statement below that best illustrates the importance of\npersonnel policies and procedures is that personnel policies\nand procedures\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","options":["Are integral to an efficient control environment.","Should be implemented where it is cost beneficial.","Should be evaluated for compliance by an external firm.","Should be implemented where risks have been identified."],"answer":"A","explanation":"In the COSO framework, the personnel policies and\nprocedures influence the control consciousness of personnel,\nso they are an integral part of an efficient control environment.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q986","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) Which one of the following statements about internal control\nrisk is correct?","options":["The establishment and maintenance of an internal control\nsystem within a company can effectively eliminate internal\ncontrol risk.","The internal audit function is responsible for the establishment\nand maintenance of a company’s internal control system.","Management has the responsibility to maintain controls that\nprovide reasonable assurance that adequate control exists\nover the entity’s assets and records.","Management’s responsibility regarding internal control risk is\nlimited to controls that provide assurance regarding the\nreliability of financial reporting."],"answer":"C","explanation":"COSO broadly defines internal control as a process, effected\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nby an entity’s board of directors, management, and other\npersonnel, designed to provide reasonable assurance\nregarding the achievement of objectives in effectiveness and\nefficiency of operations, reliability of financial reporting, and\ncompliance with applicable laws and regulations.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q987","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) Which one of the following statements is the best example of\ninherent risk?","options":["The risk that the auditor did not detect a material misstatement\nin the financial statements.","The risk that a material misstatement could occur and will not\nbe prevented.","The risk that the auditor failed to modify his or her opinion on\nmaterially misstated information.","The risk that there will be a material misstatement, assuming\nno related internal control strategy."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The risk that there will be a material misstatement, assuming\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nno related internal control strategy, is the best example of\ninherent risk. Inherent risk is the susceptibility of one of the\ncompany’s objectives to obstacles arising from the nature of\nthe objective. Inherent risk does not take internal controls into\nconsideration.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q988","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) Which of the following characteristics related to an entity’s\ncontrol environment best indicates a commitment to strong\ninternal controls?","options":["A small group of top-level executives controls decisions.","Management demonstrates independence from the board and\nexercises oversight of internal control.","The performance of individuals and teams is evaluated based\non the established standards of conduct.","The board consists of competent, experienced former senior\nmanagers of the entity."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The control environment is a set of standards, processes, and\nstructures that pervasively affects the system of internal\ncontrol. A principle that relates to the control environment is an\norganizational commitment to integrity and ethical values by\n(1) setting the tone at the top, (2) establishing standards of\nconduct, (3) evaluating the performance of individuals and\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nteams based on the established standards of conduct, and (4)\ncorrecting deviations in a timely and consistent manner.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q989","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":32,"question":"32) Which method of evaluating internal controls during the\npreliminary review provides the auditor with the best visual\ngrasp of a system and a means for analyzing complex\noperations?","options":["A flowcharting approach.","A questionnaire approach.","A matrix approach.","A detailed narrative approach.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Flowcharts are graphical representations of the step-by-step\nprogression of transactions, including document (information)\npreparation, authorization, flow, storage, etc. Flowcharting\nallows the internal auditor to analyze a system and to identify\nthe strengths and weaknesses of the purported internal\ncontrols and the appropriate areas of audit emphasis.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q990","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":33,"question":"33) The data entry staff has responsibility for converting all of a\nplant’s shipping information to computerized records. The\ninformation flow begins when the shipping department sends a\ncopy of a shipping order to the data entry staff. A data entry\noperator scans the shipping order information onto a hand-\nheld data storage device. Verification clerks then check the\ncomputerized record with the original shipping orders. When a\ngiven batch of files has been reviewed and corrected, as\nnecessary, the information is uploaded to the company’s\nmainframe system at the home office. The most effective way\nto visualize and understand this set of activities would be\nthrough the use of a","options":["Program flowchart.","Decision table.","Document flowchart.","Gantt chart.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Flowcharting is the representation of a process using pictorial\nsymbols. A document flowchart would be an effective way to\nvisualize how the document (a copy of a shipping order) flows\nthrough various departments.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q991","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":34,"question":"34) An external auditor’s primary consideration when assessing a\ncompany’s internal control structure policies and procedures is\nwhether they","options":["Prevent management override.","Relate to the control environment.","Reflect management’s philosophy and operating style.","Affect the financial statement assertions."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Management makes certain assertions about the financial\nstatements (existence, rights and obligations, etc.). The goal\nof an audit is to assess the fair presentation of the financial\nstatements. The auditor’s consideration of the client’s system\nof internal control is a means to that end.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q992","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":35,"question":"35) All of the following statements regarding the top-down or risk-\nbased approach to the audit of internal control over financial\nreporting are true except that","options":["It describes the order in which the audit procedures will be\nperformed.","The approach begins at the financial statement level.","The approach focuses on entity-level controls before moving\non to significant accounts and disclosures.","The auditor should focus on accounts and disclosures that\npresent a reasonable possibility of misstatement."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The top-down or risk-based approach to the audit of internal\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\ncontrol over financial reporting directs audit resources toward\nthose areas of the financial statements where misstatements\nare more apt to occur. This approach requires auditors to\nidentify the key day-to-day risks faced by a business, consider\nthe impact these risks could have on the financial statements,\nand plan their audit procedures accordingly. The order in\nwhich such procedures take place is not prescribed.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q993","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":36,"question":"36) A company has established policies that emphasize hiring\ncompetent people and providing them with professional\ntraining. This aligns most closely with which one of the five\nmajor components of COSO’s internal control framework?","options":["Risk assessment.","Control environment.","Monitoring.","Information and communication."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The COSO control environment is a set of standards,\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nprocesses, and structures that pervasively affects the system\nof internal control. This includes five principles: (1)\ncommitment to integrity and ethical values; (2) a board\nindependent from management that exercises oversight for\ninternal control; (3) management establishes, with board\noversight, structures, reporting lines, and appropriate\nauthorities and responsibilities; (4) a commitment to attract,\ndevelop, and retain competent individuals in alignment with\nobjectives; and (5) the organization holds individuals\naccountable for their internal control responsibilities. Thus,\nhiring competent people and providing them with training falls\nunder the control environment component of COSO’s internal\ncontrol framework.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q994","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":37,"question":"37) Accounting control should provide reasonable assurance\nabout the achievement of management’s objectives. The\nconcept of internal controls providing “reasonable assurance”\nrecognizes that","options":["The auditor’s primary responsibility is the detection of fraud.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Employee carelessness can weaken an internal accounting\ncontrol system.","Control procedures should not have an adverse effect on\nefficiency or profitability.","Judgmentally selected samples do not meet the criteria for\nstatistical validity."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Accounting control should provide reasonable assurance\nabout the achievement of management’s objectives. For\nexample, internal control objectives include improving financial\nperformance and avoiding inefficiency. Accordingly, internal\ncontrol that provides reasonable assurance of the\nachievement of the entity’s objectives should not adversely\naffect efficiency or profitability.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q995","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":38,"question":"38) In the performance of an internal audit, audit risk\nis best defined as the risk that an auditor","options":["Might not select documents that are in error as part of the\nexamination.","May not be able to properly evaluate an activity because of its\npoor internal accounting controls.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","May fail to detect a significant error or weakness during an\nexamination.","May not have the expertise to adequately audit a specific\nactivity."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Audit risk is the risk that the external auditor may unknowingly\nfail to modify his or her opinion on financial statements that\nare materially misstated. Its elements are control risk, inherent\nrisk, and detection risk. For internal auditing, the overall audit\nrisk extends not only to financial statements but also to\nunwitting failure to uncover material errors or weaknesses in\nthe operations audited. There may be several different\nreasons for the failure, and these may be in risk categories\nsuch as sampling risk, detection risk, or control risk.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q996","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":39,"question":"39) The reliability and integrity of all critical information of an\norganization, regardless of the media in which the information\nis stored, is the responsibility of","options":["Shareholders.","IT department.","Management.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","All employees."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Internal auditors determine whether senior management and\nthe board have a clear understanding that information\nreliability and integrity is a management responsibility.\nInformation reliability and integrity includes accuracy,\ncompleteness, and security.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q997","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":40,"question":"40) Which of the following factors are included in an entity’s control\nenvironment?","options":["Organizational structure, management philosophy, and\nmonitoring.","Integrity and ethical values, assignment of authority, and\nhuman resource practices.","Competence of personnel, segregation of duties, and fraud\nrisk assessment.","Risk assessment, assignment of responsibility, and human\nresource practices."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Five principles relate to the control environment. The\nprinciples are as follows: (1) the organization demonstrates a\ncommitment to integrity and ethical values; (2) the board\ndemonstrates independence from management and exercises\noversight of internal control; (3) management establishes\nstructures, reporting lines, and authorities and responsibilities;\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\n(4) the organization demonstrates a commitment to attract,\ndevelop, and retain competent individuals in alignment with\nobjectives; and (5) the organization holds individuals\naccountable for their internal control responsibilities.\nTherefore, integrity and ethical values, assignment of\nauthority, and human resource practices are factors\nconsidered in the control environment.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q998","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":41,"question":"41) Which of the following is the control component that reflects\nthe attitude and actions of the board and management\nregarding the significance of control within the organization?","options":["Risk assessment.","Control activities.","Control environment.","Monitoring."],"answer":"C","explanation":"According to the COSO model for internal control, the control\nenvironment reflects the attitude and actions of the board and\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nmanagement regarding the significance of control within the\norganization.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q999","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":42,"question":"42) The policies and procedures helping to ensure that\nmanagement directives are executed and actions are taken to\naddress risks to achievement of objectives are best described\nas","options":["Risk assessments.","Control environments.","Control activities.","Monitoring activities."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The COSO model for internal control describes control\nactivities as the policies and procedures helping to ensure that\nmanagement directives are executed and actions are taken to\naddress risks to achievement of objectives.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1000","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":43,"question":"43) An organization’s directors, management, and internal auditors\nall have important roles in creating a proper control\nenvironment. Senior management is primarily responsible for\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","options":["Establishing a proper ethical culture.","Designing and operating a control system that provides\nreasonable assurance that established objectives and goals\nwill be achieved.","Ensuring that external and internal auditors adequately monitor\nthe control environment.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Implementing and monitoring controls designed by the board\nof directors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The COSO model treats internal control as a process, effected\nby an entity’s board of directors, management, and other\npersonnel, designed to provide reasonable assurance\nregarding the achievement of entity objectives. The control\nenvironment component of internal control reflects the attitude\nand actions of the board and management regarding the\nsignificance of control within the organization. It sets the\norganization’s tone and influences the control consciousness\nof personnel. Moreover, the control environment provides\ndiscipline and structure for the achievement of the primary\nobjectives of internal control. The control environment\nincludes, among other elements, integrity and ethical values.\nThus, standards should be effectively communicated, e.g., by\nmanagement example. Management also should remove\nincentives and temptations for dishonest or unethical acts.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1001","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":44,"question":"44) Which one of the following statements in regard to internal\ncontrols is not true?","options":["Control environment is the foundation for all other components\nof internal control, providing discipline and structure.","Control activities are the policies and procedures that help\nensure that management directives are carried out.","Monitoring is a process that penalizes managers for\nbreakdowns in internal control.","Risk assessment is the entity’s identification and analysis of\nrelevant risks to achievement of its objectives.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Monitoring is a process that assesses the quality of internal\ncontrol performance over time to ensure that controls continue\nto meet the needs of the organization. The organization\nevaluates and communicates control deficiencies based on\nwhat is found when monitoring. Monitoring should be used to\nensure internal controls are designed and operating\neffectively, not as a tool to assign blame to management.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1002","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":45,"question":"45) Which one of the following auditor findings would most likely\nraise a red flag about a company’s internal control\nenvironment?","options":["The company has an established independent audit\ncommittee.","Only select committees of the board have access to outside\nattorneys.","The role of the company’s CEO and board chairman are\nseparate.","The board nominations committee selects only independent\ndirectors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Although the need for attorneys itself does not raise any red\nflags, the fact that not all committees have access to attorneys\nmay indicate a reason for concern. Such a policy may grant\nthose committees with access an unintended amount of\nauthority or power, which may lead to fraudulent acts.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1003","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":46,"question":"46) The risk associated with auditors failing to identify material\nmisstatements in a financial statement is referred to as","options":["Control risk.","Inherent risk.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Unsystematic risk.","Detection risk."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Detection risk is the risk that an obstacle to an objective will\nnot be detected before a loss has occurred.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1004","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":47,"question":"47) Which of the following statements regarding the\nCOSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework is correct?","options":["The framework was published by ISACA.","The COSO Enterprise Risk Management framework was\nupdated to become the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework.","Publicly-listed companies are required by regulation to apply\nthe framework in designing their internal control systems.","The framework includes objectives, components, and related\nprinciples."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework consists\nprimarily of a definition of internal control, categories of\nobjectives, components and related principles, and\nrequirements of an effective system of internal control.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1005","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":48,"question":"48) The COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework includes a\ndefinition of","options":["Internal control and requirements of an efficient internal control\nsystem.","Data governance and requirements of an effective IT control\nsystem.","Internal auditing and requirements of an effective internal\ncontrol system.","Internal control and requirements of an effective internal\ncontrol system."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework consists\nprimarily of a definition of internal control, categories of\nobjectives, components and related principles, and\nrequirements of an effective system of internal control.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1006","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":49,"question":"49) Which of the following is a component of internal control\naccording to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework?","options":["Performance.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Review and revision.","Information and communication.","Governance and culture."],"answer":"C","explanation":"According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, the components of internal control consist of the\ncontrol environment, risk assessment, control activities,\ninformation and communication, and monitoring.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1007","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":50,"question":"50) Which of the following are components of internal control\naccording to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework?","options":["Compliance, operations, and reporting.","Principles, framework, and process.","Monitoring, control activities, and risk assessment.","Function, division, and operating unit.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"C","explanation":"According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, the components of internal control consist of the\ncontrol environment, risk assessment, control activities,\ninformation and communication, and monitoring.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1008","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":51,"question":"51) A system of internal control is effective if it provides\nreasonable assurance of achieving an entity’s objectives.\nWhich of the following is an objective specified in the\nCOSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework?","options":["Innovation.","Operations.","Risk assessment.","Productivity."],"answer":"B","explanation":"According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, a system of internal control is effective if it\nprovides reasonable assurance of achieving an entity’s\nobjectives relating to operations, reporting, and compliance.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1009","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":52,"question":"52) A system of internal control is effective if it provides\nreasonable assurance of achieving an entity’s objectives.\nWhich of the following are objectives specified in the\nCOSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework?","options":["Internal, external, and overall.","Specific, measurable, and obtainable.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Compliance, operations, and reporting.","Strategic, compliance, and operations."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Internal Control – Integrated Framework, a system of internal\ncontrol is effective if it provides reasonable assurance of\nachieving an entity’s objectives relating to operations,\nreporting, and compliance.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1010","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":53,"question":"53) Which of the following are included in an entity’s organizational\nstructure according to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework cube?","options":["Operations, reporting, and compliance.","Compliance, risk management, and internal audit.","Entity level, division, and operating unit.","Function, department, and subsidiary."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework can be\nrepresented by a cube with rows, slices, and columns. The\nrows are the five components, the slices are the three\nobjectives, and the columns are an entity’s organizational\nstructure, which includes entity level, division, operating unit,\nand function.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1011","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":54,"question":"54) Which of the following is part of an entity’s organizational\nstructure according to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework cube?","options":["Process.","Function.","Activity.","Transaction."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework can be\nrepresented by a cube with rows, slices, and columns. The\nrows are the five components, the slices are the three\nobjectives, and the columns are an entity’s organizational\nstructure, which includes entity level, division, operating unit,\nand function.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1012","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":55,"question":"55) Which of the following is part of an entity’s organizational\nstructure according to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework cube?","options":["Process.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Function.","Activity.","Transaction."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework can be\nrepresented by a cube with rows, slices, and columns. The\nrows are the five components, the slices are the three\nobjectives, and the columns are an entity’s organizational\nstructure, which includes entity level, division, operating unit,\nand function.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1013","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":56,"question":"56) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, which of the following lists an example of a\ncomponent, entity structure, and objective, respectively?","options":["Risk assessment, operations, and reporting.","Monitoring, entity level, and compliance.","Capability, operating unit, and control.","Control environment, function, and learning."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Within the COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework, the\ncomponents are control environment, risk assessment, control\nactivities, information and communication, and monitoring;\nentity structure includes entity level, division, operating unit,\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nand function; and objectives include operations, reporting, and\ncompliance.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1014","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":57,"question":"57) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, compliance, information and communication, and\nfunction, respectively, are examples of what aspects of the\nCOSO cube?","options":["Objective, component, and entity structure.","Entity structure, component, and division.","Component, principle, and entity structure.","Objective, objective, and entity structure.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Within the COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework,\ncomponents include control environment, risk assessment,\ncontrol activities, information and communication, and\nmonitoring; entity structure includes entity level, division,\noperating unit, and function; and objectives include operations,\nreporting, and compliance.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1015","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":58,"question":"58) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, which of the following terms refers to the\ndetermination that internal control components and relevant\nprinciples exist in the design and implementation of the internal\ncontrol system?","options":["Present.","Efficient.","Functioning.","Operating together."],"answer":"A","explanation":"An effective system of internal control requires that each of the\nfive components of internal control and the relevant principles\nis present and functioning. “Present” refers to whether the\ncomponents and relevant principles exist in the design and\nimplementation of an internal control system.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1016","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":59,"question":"59) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, which of the following terms refers to the\ndetermination that internal control components and relevant\nprinciples continue to exist in the operation of an internal\ncontrol system?","options":["Effective.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Functioning.","Operating together.","Present."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An effective system of internal control requires that each of the\nfive components of internal control and the relevant principles\nis present and functioning. “Functioning” refers to whether the\ncomponents and relevant principles continue to exist in the\noperation of an internal control system.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1017","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":60,"question":"60) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, which of the following terms refers to the\ndetermination that all internal control components collectively\nreduce the risk of not achieving an objective to an acceptable\nlevel?","options":["Present.","Operating together.","Adequate.","Functioning."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An effective system of internal control requires that each of the\nfive components of internal control and the relevant principles\nis present and functioning. The five components should\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\noperate together in an integrated manner. “Operating\ntogether” refers to whether all five components collectively\nreduce the risk of not achieving an objective to an acceptable\nlevel.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1018","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":61,"question":"61) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, an effective internal control system requires that","options":["Internal control components collectively eliminate the risk of\nnot achieving an objective.","Each of the internal control components and corresponding\nobjectives is present and functioning.","Each of the internal control components is operating\nindependently.","Each of the internal control components and relevant\nprinciples is present and functioning."],"answer":"D","explanation":"According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, an effective internal control system requires that\neach of the five components of internal control and the\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\nrelevant principles is present and functioning. The five\ncomponents should operate together in an integrated manner.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1019","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":62,"question":"62) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, which of the following statements is correct?","options":["The principles in the framework change with the application of\nemerging technology.","The use of outsourced service providers relieves the\norganization’s responsibility for its internal control.","The framework requires judgment in designing and conducting\ninternal control.","The framework is designed for larger, not smaller,\norganizations."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The use of judgment is required when designing,\nimplementing, and conducting internal control.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1020","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":63,"question":"63) Which of the following is a component of internal control\ndefined by the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework?","options":["Internal environment.\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control","Control environment.","Objective setting.","Strategy."],"answer":"B","explanation":"According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, the components of internal control consist of the\ncontrol environment, risk assessment, control activities,\ninformation and communication, and monitoring.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1021","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":64,"question":"64) Which of the following are components of internal control\ndefined by the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework?","options":["Event identification, information and communication, and risk\nassessment.","Internal environment, control activities, and monitoring.","Information and communication, control environment, and\ncontrol activities.","Governance, risk appetite, and control environment."],"answer":"C","explanation":"According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, the components of internal control consist of the\n\n2: Risk and Internal Control\ncontrol environment, risk assessment, control activities,\ninformation and communication, and monitoring.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"v2-su17-q1022","volume":2,"unit":17,"unitTitle":"Internal Controls — Corporate Governance","subunit":null,"questionNumber":65,"question":"65) According to the COSO Internal Control – Integrated\nFramework, which of the following lists provides an example of\nan objective, component, and entity structure?\nObjectives Components Entity structure","options":["Customization Risk assessment Entity level","Operations Monitoring and review Function","Reporting Control activities Division","Compliance Value creation Division\n\nSubunit 1: Control Procedures\n18: (149) Internal Controls -- Controls and Security\nMeasures\n1: (89) Control Procedures\n2: (39) Systems Controls and Information Security\n3: (21) Security Measures and Business Continuity\nPlanning\n18: (149) Internal Controls -- Controls and Security\nMeasures\n1: (89) Control Procedures\nSubunit 1: Control Procedures"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Within the COSO Internal Control – Integrated Framework,\nobjectives include operations, reporting, and compliance;\ncomponents include the control environment, risk assessment,\ncontrol activities, information and communication, and\nmonitoring; and entity structure includes the entity level,\ndivision, operating unit, and function.","sectionId":"17.2"},{"id":"aq.ext.aud.req.003_1802","question":"Inherent risk and control risk differ from detection risk in that they:","options":["Arise from the misapplication of auditing procedures.","May be assessed in only quantitative terms.","Exist independently of the financial statement audit.","Can be changed at the auditor's discretion."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is correct. AU 312 states that inherent risk and control risk exist independently of the audit, whereas detection risk relates to the auditor's procedures and can be changed at his discretion.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ext.aud.req.006_1802","question":"If there is appropriate evidence to conclude that the financial statements are free from material misstatement, but there are going-concern doubts, what is the most appropriate audit opinion?","options":["An unmodified opinion","An unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter or other-matter paragraph","A qualified opinion","A disclaimer of opinion"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is correct. Going-concern doubts are one of the issues that should be brought to the attention of financial statement users even if there is appropriate evidence to conclude that the financial statements are free from material misstatements. Other issues that should be brought to the attention of the financial statement users include inconsistent application of accounting principles, uncertainty about significant future events such as the outcome of pending litigation, and a change in opinion for a prior period that is included in the current financial statements or the predecessor auditor's report.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ext.aud.req.005_1802","question":"In which case would an auditor give an adverse opinion?","options":["When the auditors have evidence that the financial statements are materially and pervasively misstated.","When auditors have examined a complete set of financial statements and have gained sufficient appropriate evidence that the financial statements are free from material misstatement.","When the auditor is unable to obtain sufficient audit evidence to make an opinion regarding the reliability of the financial statements.","When auditors feel they can't issue an unqualified opinion."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is correct. This option correctly describes a situation when an auditor would give an adverse opinion after auditing the financial statements.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ext.aud.req.004_1802","question":"An auditor uses the assessed level of control risk to:","options":["Evaluate the effectiveness of the entity's internal control policies and procedures.","Identify transactions and account balances where inherent risk is at the maximum.","Indicate whether materiality thresholds for planning and evaluation purposes are sufficiently high.","Determine the acceptable level of detection risk for financial statement assertions."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This is correct. According to the audit risk model, acceptable detection risk is a function of allowable audit risk, inherent risk, and control risk.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ext.aud.req.002_1802","question":"The most important distinction between auditing standards (AS) issued by the PCAOB and statements on auditing standards (SAS) issued by the ASB is:","options":["Non-issuers are not permitted to be audited under AS issued by PCAOB, but issuers are permitted to be audited under SAS issued by ASB.","AS issued by the PCAOB apply only to issuers.","All SAS issued by the ASB also apply to audits of issuers, but not all AS issued by the PCAOB are applicable to non-issuers.","There are now only minor differences between AS and SAS."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is correct. Auditing standards are required to be applied to all issuers while statements on auditing standards apply to audits of non-issuers.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ext.aud.req.001_1802","question":"According to the PCAOB auditing standards (AS), the auditor of a company that issues securities must audit the company's internal control, as well as its financial statements. What is the recommended timing of these two audits?","options":["The two audits should be integrated.","The internal control audit should be performed first and be immediately followed by the audit of the company's financial statements.","The financial statement audit should be performed first and be immediately followed by the audit of the company's internal control.","The internal control audit should be performed first, unless there is an adequate reason for first performing the financial statement audit."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is correct. The PCAOB has recommended that the two audits should be integrated as much as possible, in part to reduce both time and cost.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.003_1802","question":"The Committee of Sponsoring Organizations (COSO) internal control framework consists of five interrelated components. Which of the following is not one of these components?","options":["Risk assessment","Information and communication","Management risk","Monitoring"],"answer":"C","explanation":"This answer is correct. The five interrelated components are the control environment, risk assessment, control activities, information and communication, and monitoring.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.005_1802","question":"Management philosophy and operating style would most likely have a significant influence on the entity's control environment when:","options":["Management is dominated by one individual.","Accurate management job descriptions delineate specific duties.","The audit committee actively oversees the financial reporting process.","The internal auditor reports directly to management."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer is correct. Management is responsible for the establishment and ongoing supervision of internal control. When management is dominated by one individual, the internal control is significantly influenced by that individual and the credibility of internal control is quite often considered impaired.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.001_1802","question":"Which of the following is not an objective of an entity's internal control system?","options":["To prevent collusion with respect to overriding the internal control system","To provide reasonable assurance that the entity's goals are being met","To increase the reliability of the financial reporting process","To provide reasonable assurance that there is compliance with certain legal obligations"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer is correct. An internal control system is not designed to prevent collusion.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.006_1802","question":"Which of the following components of an entity's internal control includes the development of personnel manuals documenting employee promotion and training policies?","options":["Risk assessment","Control environment","Control activities","Monitoring"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is correct. The control environment has to do with the attitude and culture toward internal controls. It also includes human resource policies and practices. Manuals documenting employee promotion and training policies would be included as part of human resources under the control environment.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.007_1802","question":"Companies with international operations must comply with the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (FCPA). In order to comply with FCPA, a company's internal controls must provide reasonable assurance that:","options":["Management must authorize all access to assets.","The company's financial records are in accordance with applicable accounting standards.","Management must authorize all access to assets and that the company's financial records are in accordance with applicable accounting standards.","Management does not need to authorize all access to assets and the company's financial records are not required to be in accordance with applicable accounting standards."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is correct. Companies with international operations that must comply with the FCPA must have internal controls that provide reasonable assurance that management must authorize all access to assets and that the company's financial records are in accordance with applicable accounting standards.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.002_1802","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the development and implementation of an internal control system is correct?","options":["The reliability of the financial statements is assured.","Information should be identified, captured, and communicated in a timely manner.","The success with respect to an entity achieving its goals is assured.","The external auditors will develop the internal control system of a public company."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. One result of developing and implementing an internal control system is that information should be identified, captured, and communicated in a timely manner.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.phil.004_1802","question":"The overall attitude and awareness of an entity's board of directors concerning the importance of internal control usually is reflected in its:","options":["Computer-based controls.","Safeguards over access to assets.","System of segregation of duties.","Control environment."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This answer is correct. The control environment is one of the five interrelated components of an internal control system. The control environment reflects the overall attitude, awareness, and actions of the owners, board of directors, management, and others concerning the importance of internal control within the entity.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.003_1802","question":"Which board of directors committee is charged with overseeing the financial reporting process?","options":["The audit committee","The compensation committee","The financial committee","The governance committee"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is correct. The audit committee is charged with overseeing the financial reporting process.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.006_1802","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the Sarbanes-Oxley Act requirements with respect to auditor rotation?","options":["The audit firm must rotate the lead audit partner every five years.","The company being audited must rotate the audit firm every five years.","The audit firm must rotate the audit staff every five years.","The company being audited must rotate the audit committee every five years."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is correct. This requirement is described in Section 201 of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.001_1802","question":"How entities are systematically directed and controlled refers to which of the following?","options":["Corporate governance","Sustainability","Agents","Internal controls"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is correct. Corporate governance refers to how entities are systematically directed and controlled, including the oversight and obligations of the board of directors, management, and the audit committee.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.007_1802","question":"The Sarbanes-Oxley Act disallows each of the following services for auditors of a publicly traded company except for:","options":["Human resource services.","Financial information systems design.","Tax preparation services.","Actuarial services."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is correct. Tax preparation services are not prohibited under the services that the Sarbanes-Oxley Act disallows to be provided to an audit client.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.005_1802","question":"The CEO of American Foods has made a variety of significant investments that have changed the firm's focus and diminished share value. One of American Foods’ shareholders has aggressively been acquiring shares and now owns 10% of the company. The shareholder has nominated Erik Sorenson to the board of directors in an attempt to oversee manager actions. Although Sorenson could ask for the manager's resignation, he prefers that the manager make choices that enhance firm value. This type of motivation is known as:","options":["Performance shares.","Executive stock options.","Direct intervention.","The threat of firing."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This is correct. Sorenson can fire the manager. If shareholders are unsatisfied with the results, they can replace board members and have an influence on who holds manager positions.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.002_1802","question":"Which of the following is not an attribute required by the Sarbanes Oxley Act for the financial expert who serves on the board of directors and the board's audit committee?","options":["An understanding of internal controls","The individual must be a CPA or CFA","An understanding of audit committee functions","The ability to assess the accounting for accruals, estimates, and reserves"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is correct. There is no requirement that an individual who is the financial expert is either a CPA or CFA.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.corp.gov.004_1802","question":"Which of the following does not have to be certified by the management of a public company according to the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)?","options":["Management's responsibility for the internal control system.","The financial reports have been reviewed by management.","Management has certified that the auditor has reviewed the internal control system.","Management has had discussions with the external audit firm and the audit committee with respect to any material internal control weaknesses."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is correct. Management is not responsible for certifying that the auditor has reviewed the internal control system; the external auditor is responsible for this.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.risk.001_1802","question":"It is important for the independent auditor to consider the competence of the audit client's employees because their competence bears directly on the:","options":["Achievement of the objectives of the system of internal control.","Cost/benefit relationship of the system of internal control.","Comparison of stated employee responsibility and accountability with what has been achieved.","Timing of the tests to be performed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is correct. With reasonably competent employees and clear lines of authority and responsibility, there is a high probability that the stated objectives of the system of internal control will have been accomplished.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.risk.005_1802","question":"Which of the following best describes when you would want to use insurance as a risk management strategy?","options":["Situations with Low Potential Loss and Low Likelihood","Situations with High Potential Loss and Low Likelihood","Situations with Low Potential Loss and High Likelihood","Situations with High Potential Loss and High Likelihood"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is correct. Purchasing insurance is the best risk management strategy for situations with high potential loss and low likelihood of occurrence.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.risk.003_1802","question":"Which of the following is true of detective controls?","options":["Detective controls are only designed to prevent control failures from occurring.","Detective controls are designed both to detect control failures after they occur and to reverse or reduce the errors that are found.","Detective controls are only designed to detect control failures after they occur.","None of the options are correct."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is correct. Detective controls are designed to detect control failures after they occur.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.risk.004_1802","question":"In a situation like employees stealing cash from a cash register, what would be the mostappropriate risk management strategy?","options":["Accept risk.","Avoid risk.","Purchase insurance.","Implement controls."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This is correct. An event like the one mentioned above represents a low potential loss, but high likelihood of such an event occurring. According to the risk matrix, the best strategy for this situation would be to implement controls.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ic.risk.002_1802","question":"An auditor uses the knowledge provided by the understanding of internal controls and the assessed level of control risk primarily to:","options":["Determine whether the control procedures and supporting records concerning the safeguarding of assets are reliable.","Determine the nature, timing, and extent of further audit procedures.","Modify the preliminary judgments about materiality levels.","Modify the initial assessments of inherent risk."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is correct. An auditor uses the knowledge provided by the understanding of internal controls and the assessed level of control risk primarily for the reasons mentioned here.","unit":17,"subunit":"17.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0}];